#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186156984 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f1059ab6-d512-537f-be1e-28aa492a6ef0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Suitable Variation of Parameters Ansatz"}] :question/id #uuid "6a492cbf-d378-5193-baf2-c88a2bea63fb" :question/math-academy-id "e-21774" :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} - 6y = (3x+1)\\sin(x).\n$$\n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, what is a suitable ansatz for the particular solution $y_p(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution $y_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\ny_c' - 6y_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c  = Ae^{6x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable $y_p,$ we set $A = u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = u(x)e^{6x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of $y_p$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186156985 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7ac34a18-053d-5eab-8c2c-bd0f15658186" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order Linear ODEs Using Variation of Parameters"}] :question/id #uuid "9a26313d-4b93-5d82-98c1-dbecb0bead82" :question/math-academy-id "e-21775" :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} - 5y = \\cos 2x.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\qquad$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form  ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/assets/ae29bceba4d6a1f250f244b26583abeb4b8c2fbe33d380dfa4332b051bb21078.png).\n\n$\\qquad$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x) =$ ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/assets/c6725d6cd732c1ca7f21ab9a8dac245bc396c04a623cfb8e2eda31751ddaa89e.png).\n\n$\\qquad$ Therefore, the particular solution is $y_p(x) =$  ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/assets/1d1c8a545fb741413f7cdd2f6f3fa6190fbdda74290e206cc4a7f8061f97ff53.png)." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution $y_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\ny_c' - 5y_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c  = Ae^{5x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable $y_p,$ we set $A = u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = u(x)e^{5x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of $y_p$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate $u'{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{5x})}^{′}-5(u{e}^{5x}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{5x}+5u{e}^{5x}-5u{e}^{5x} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{5x}+\\cancel{5u{e}^{5x}}-\\cancel{5u{e}^{5x}} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{5x} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\ {u}^{′} & ={e}^{-5x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & ={e}^{-5x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{-5x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =\\int {e}^{-5x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $y_p = u(x)e^{5x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{5x} \\\\  & =\\int {e}^{-5x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {e}^{5x} \\\\  & ={e}^{5x}\\int {e}^{-5x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186156986 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5fb547a-2518-5db9-b1b1-aab8d80328af" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order Linear ODEs Using VOP When the Forcing Term is Part of the Complementary Solution"}] :question/id #uuid "bafa3bf1-a867-5259-a6da-226195b45541" :question/math-academy-id "e-21777" :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} - 3y = 12\\sinh 3x.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\qquad$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form  ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/assets/ae29bceba4d6a1f250f244b26583abeb4b8c2fbe33d380dfa4332b051bb21078.png).\n\n$\\qquad$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x) =$  ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/assets/c6725d6cd732c1ca7f21ab9a8dac245bc396c04a623cfb8e2eda31751ddaa89e.png).\n\n$\\qquad$ Therefore, the particular solution is $y_p(x) =$  ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/assets/1d1c8a545fb741413f7cdd2f6f3fa6190fbdda74290e206cc4a7f8061f97ff53.png)." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution $y_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\ny_c' - 3y_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c  = Ae^{3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable $y_p,$ we set $A = u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = u(x)e^{3x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of $y_p$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate $u'{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{3x})}^{′}-3(u{e}^{3x}) & =12\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{3x}+3u{e}^{3x}-3u{e}^{3x} & =12\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{3x}+\\cancel{3u{e}^{3x}}-\\cancel{3u{e}^{3x}} & =12\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{3x} & =12\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x \\\\ {u}^{′} & =12{e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\sinh 3x = \\dfrac{e^{3x} - e^{-3x}}{2}\n$$\n\nour equation for $u$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =12{e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x \\\\  & =12{e}^{-3x}(\\frac{{e}^{3x}-{e}^{-3x}}{2}) \\\\  & =6{e}^{-3x}({e}^{3x}-{e}^{-3x}) \\\\  & =6-6{e}^{-6x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =6-6{e}^{-6x} \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 6-6{e}^{-6x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =6x+{e}^{-6x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $y_p = u(x)e^{3x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{3x} \\\\  & =6x{e}^{3x}+{e}^{-3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157695 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7ac34a18-053d-5eab-8c2c-bd0f15658186" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order Linear ODEs Using Variation of Parameters"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "841b977e-80d4-5643-a50c-d00e3917bb74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x)e^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155374} {:answer/id #uuid "c7a79cea-33fe-5602-938c-728bdaa6b324" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x)e^{− 2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155375} {:answer/id #uuid "efb7d0f1-d310-52a8-97e8-7ffb93cbb32a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x)e^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155376} {:answer/id #uuid "0ff8e560-14df-565d-8e02-7d9a1f3cc6f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x)e^{− 4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155377} {:answer/id #uuid "30ecd628-e3df-5f9c-8b57-329a0da8b0f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x)e^{6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155378}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ff8e560-14df-565d-8e02-7d9a1f3cc6f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x)e^{− 4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155377} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f3511e1-d6c3-5d05-9adf-3ff8bc8cfca0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155373} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e22471a-3a2e-5481-a210-ff1bf839c5e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1)/(2)e^{− 6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155380} {:answer/id #uuid "1a101238-ee20-58a8-a2a3-c031acbb7d15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1)/(6)e^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155381} {:answer/id #uuid "5bf20ab6-8cb6-5e60-9e7e-c6fa96a6d8a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1)/(6)e^{− 10x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155382} {:answer/id #uuid "6280a3f9-8a37-534e-b115-0b81009fe491" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1)/(4)e^{− 2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155383} {:answer/id #uuid "7d2f3112-2b82-5ef3-97dc-691896201a6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1)/(4)e^{− 8x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155384}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a101238-ee20-58a8-a2a3-c031acbb7d15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1)/(6)e^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155381} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ae6ccf3-23a9-5dcb-91eb-f621f369c289" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155379} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f6c0d6e4-9aec-54cd-ac5e-ce08140686c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1)/(2)e^{− 2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155386} {:answer/id #uuid "02eaff40-3199-5e64-a8c2-a4ea101fcef8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1)/(6)e^{− 6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155387} {:answer/id #uuid "1c9e6c83-dad4-5542-8bc7-d6fdad132da3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1)/(4)e^{− 4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155388} {:answer/id #uuid "301692b7-fe97-5f55-a325-9a32defb42bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1)/(4)e^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155389} {:answer/id #uuid "4cf2fd77-d42b-50aa-a71d-783b01855177" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1)/(6)e^{6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155390}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4cf2fd77-d42b-50aa-a71d-783b01855177" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1)/(6)e^{6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155390} :answer-field/id #uuid "51d78068-0f09-56a1-82bf-40b753bb93e2" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155385}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "62e0a162-13e2-59de-92b2-13df782b20dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-337174" :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4y={e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below. \n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+4{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-4x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-4x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{-4x})}^{′}+4(u{e}^{-4x}) & ={e}^{2x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-4x}-4u{e}^{-4x}+4u{e}^{-4x} & ={e}^{2x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-4x}-\\cancel{4u{e}^{-4x}}+\\cancel{4u{e}^{-4x}} & ={e}^{2x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-4x} & ={e}^{2x} \\\\ {u}^{′} & ={e}^{6x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & ={e}^{6x} \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{6x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =\\frac{1}{6}{e}^{6x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{-4x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{-4x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{e}^{6x}\\cdot {e}^{-4x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157696 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7ac34a18-053d-5eab-8c2c-bd0f15658186" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order Linear ODEs Using Variation of Parameters"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91dcfc07-49d0-5122-94a9-06c35d2d5b5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x)e^{− 4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155398} {:answer/id #uuid "94a59549-1a48-59f2-802c-2646623d7440" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x)e^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155399} {:answer/id #uuid "9d534a6e-134a-5e12-96a0-1cfcdb092423" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x)e^{− 2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155400} {:answer/id #uuid "ccd82c5b-3414-5b49-a82d-5791afc0a5c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x)e^{6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155401} {:answer/id #uuid "124d697f-4c37-5288-88a9-d932ced8c6d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x)e^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155402}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "94a59549-1a48-59f2-802c-2646623d7440" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x)e^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155399} :answer-field/id #uuid "62aa8e6e-68b0-591c-ae1f-be02e42a4069" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155397} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "886f45b3-41db-5a5c-9653-b06f1a3dd432" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322440} {:answer/id #uuid "6be492da-4423-5ee0-b031-0265fdc21c45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322441} {:answer/id #uuid "21f3913e-8266-54c7-9844-71f0610500eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322442} {:answer/id #uuid "e5cc2058-45a4-5086-9cd1-e0b3d99a688c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322443}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "886f45b3-41db-5a5c-9653-b06f1a3dd432" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322440} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc1b58f9-6fec-5045-a2c7-9a63cd9e58e0" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322439} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cc4b4252-df0a-5df9-b823-51f54141f5e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-2x}\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322445} {:answer/id #uuid "3d10d602-bb3c-5cc5-bacb-485f6bb9dd0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2x}\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322446} {:answer/id #uuid "3adfb6c9-3907-5f9f-8c58-0619ce991c87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-2x}\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322447} {:answer/id #uuid "a0a48145-8995-5147-87e2-508318b27153" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2x}\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322448}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0a48145-8995-5147-87e2-508318b27153" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2x}\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322448} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f98727d-5040-529a-b72d-f49b8a4ec8e9" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322444}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "46fd6bfd-4101-5948-91fa-bae3c4ec8a60" :question/math-academy-id "q-337175" :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below. \n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-2{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{2x})}^{′}-2(u{e}^{2x}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{2x}+2u{e}^{2x}-2u{e}^{2x} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{2x}+\\cancel{2u{e}^{2x}}-\\cancel{2u{e}^{2x}} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{2x} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x \\\\ {u}^{′} & ={e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & ={e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{2x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{2x} \\\\  & =\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {e}^{2x} \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157698 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5fb547a-2518-5db9-b1b1-aab8d80328af" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order Linear ODEs Using VOP When the Forcing Term is Part of the Complementary Solution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a1034da1-ae39-5071-beb9-d524c91b3460" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x)e^{6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155428} {:answer/id #uuid "ad718114-0514-5c21-9296-0a1cb9a1d0bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x)e^{− 4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155429} {:answer/id #uuid "f723a595-68b1-5b1c-b418-3f893ec254a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x)e^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155430} {:answer/id #uuid "ff834ebb-28f4-5f0e-adbb-4ac4dc78a4be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x)e^{− 2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155431} {:answer/id #uuid "1e6783e9-23fd-51be-9ee6-57060434f79f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x)e^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155432}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e6783e9-23fd-51be-9ee6-57060434f79f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x)e^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155432} :answer-field/id #uuid "05ea8b44-cc8a-5121-9427-a141af1d5b6c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155427} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2eeb174-374e-5dde-a1f7-1f6c1c0574dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{2x} − xe^{− 2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155434} {:answer/id #uuid "bad49942-1371-5682-ac55-d9bceb478c26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2xe^{2x} − (1)/(2)e^{− 2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155435} {:answer/id #uuid "4027305d-c9ab-54aa-922c-545cbd7965cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{2x} − (1)/(2)xe^{− 2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155436} {:answer/id #uuid "52808803-ab22-527c-9dae-e4268b808126" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2xe^{2x} − e^{− 2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155437}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bad49942-1371-5682-ac55-d9bceb478c26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2xe^{2x} − (1)/(2)e^{− 2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155435} :answer-field/id #uuid "e02ff2b8-a01d-58aa-9cc7-2d41c43659a9" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155433} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2736ff03-1c98-5b68-85fe-187816f437aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1 − xe^{− 4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155439} {:answer/id #uuid "2d04e064-35cf-5a96-8bb9-b6026ec552bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x − (1)/(2)e^{− 4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155440} {:answer/id #uuid "2dbceb89-b7d8-50be-b999-bf77ac3a7f2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x − e^{− 4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155441} {:answer/id #uuid "6641ad8d-eb3e-544b-860e-ac02db841ea4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1 − (1)/(2)xe^{− 4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155442}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d04e064-35cf-5a96-8bb9-b6026ec552bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x − (1)/(2)e^{− 4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155440} :answer-field/id #uuid "c14cc095-85ab-58c8-9872-846e62eccb43" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155438}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "89999e08-d505-549a-bd78-727ab6426321" :question/math-academy-id "q-337179" :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2y=4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below. \n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-2{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{2x})}^{′}-2(u{e}^{2x}) & =4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{2x}+2u{e}^{2x}-2u{e}^{2x} & =4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{2x}+\\cancel{2u{e}^{2x}}-\\cancel{2u{e}^{2x}} & =4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{2x} & =4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x \\\\ {u}^{′} & =4{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x=\\frac{{e}^{2x}+{e}^{-2x}}{2}\n$$\n\nour equation for $u$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =4{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x \\\\  & =4{e}^{-2x}(\\frac{{e}^{2x}+{e}^{-2x}}{2}) \\\\  & =2{e}^{-2x}({e}^{2x}+{e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =2+2{e}^{-4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =2+2{e}^{-4x} \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 2+2{e}^{-4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =2x-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{2x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{2x} \\\\  & =2x{e}^{2x}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322449 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f1059ab6-d512-537f-be1e-28aa492a6ef0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Suitable Variation of Parameters Ansatz"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af088a40-016c-57be-a1ca-8a647c105b32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-7x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322451} {:answer/id #uuid "3bae8f30-a9b1-523b-8750-02bee38eec87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322452} {:answer/id #uuid "8ecf2147-42c8-5d9d-ad60-389fe599b6b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322453} {:answer/id #uuid "d7d090ca-b096-54c3-97a4-0221e7d1f597" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322454} {:answer/id #uuid "cd750656-25d0-52a3-a735-98ca4eab9ab2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322455}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8ecf2147-42c8-5d9d-ad60-389fe599b6b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322453} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7e8a7d9-bc76-5e2f-ab40-b8c6ee312d81" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322450}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "38ecf25c-c9d5-534a-b7ae-492cd6b26809" :question/math-academy-id "q-337274" :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4y=({x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, which of the following is a suitable ansatz for the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+4{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-4x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-4x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322456 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f1059ab6-d512-537f-be1e-28aa492a6ef0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Suitable Variation of Parameters Ansatz"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff4a4562-d477-51c8-a983-d47a0c6f43a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322458} {:answer/id #uuid "085a4b8f-12c9-5023-82fe-a16ca10584c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322459} {:answer/id #uuid "f6c581de-c7f4-5505-86cc-e2877f1cf919" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322460} {:answer/id #uuid "9a3d5e73-d47f-5277-81fd-8178eed59146" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322461} {:answer/id #uuid "75b19826-cf2e-5d9c-95ba-b5bca7f20ece" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322462}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "085a4b8f-12c9-5023-82fe-a16ca10584c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322459} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0d75639-3264-520d-aa8b-5d9cb44b6eef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322457}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8282d8ad-97bf-5389-8dc6-99efa6d356bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-337276" :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-5y=(2x-7)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, which of the following is a suitable ansatz for the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-5{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{5x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{5x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322463 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f1059ab6-d512-537f-be1e-28aa492a6ef0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Suitable Variation of Parameters Ansatz"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36ffded6-ee5e-5e1f-8d0e-a89f6c288b8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322465} {:answer/id #uuid "c7bb1a00-6ee8-541c-9610-7657d273bdf1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322466} {:answer/id #uuid "fe062085-7db9-5a01-8166-ecc75fb513dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322467} {:answer/id #uuid "56192755-24a1-54b2-bbe6-36fa4a18e2f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322468} {:answer/id #uuid "2a03d023-d77e-5c70-9699-365496399d23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322469}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a03d023-d77e-5c70-9699-365496399d23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322469} :answer-field/id #uuid "4aa480f9-7b8e-5e27-ba9f-b3a892e3769c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322464}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e558b463-2e7c-56f1-ac9d-9bb2b79dfcf2" :question/math-academy-id "q-337278" :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y={x}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, which of the following is a suitable ansatz for the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322470 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f1059ab6-d512-537f-be1e-28aa492a6ef0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Suitable Variation of Parameters Ansatz"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32d0b9b2-005c-588b-85de-d9bbf7b93911" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322472} {:answer/id #uuid "df52aaee-1dc7-5b23-a4d7-1f7978c7a47d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322473} {:answer/id #uuid "ceb135fb-2497-5e3b-bd52-5d52eaca41c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322474} {:answer/id #uuid "f4ff2732-995c-50a6-bdd5-23e1c6273404" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322475} {:answer/id #uuid "47a59205-0460-599e-a0f0-db392fd25fba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322476}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df52aaee-1dc7-5b23-a4d7-1f7978c7a47d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322473} :answer-field/id #uuid "118c3ef4-0284-5972-81aa-48fc960a8127" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322471}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b4126f36-d39e-51f5-bf3c-6234278051da" :question/math-academy-id "q-337356" :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3y=({x}^{2}+2)\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, which of the following is a suitable ansatz for the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-3{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322477 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f1059ab6-d512-537f-be1e-28aa492a6ef0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Suitable Variation of Parameters Ansatz"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "643e793d-c597-5a91-8005-379a40c51f62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322479} {:answer/id #uuid "c2889bf1-5415-5c11-9b10-8f5d7f6fb564" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322480} {:answer/id #uuid "4bf9c4d9-b4c3-5add-a80a-8a048dba3f92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322481} {:answer/id #uuid "4e370238-d05a-5814-9221-1fd8179787b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322482} {:answer/id #uuid "035b66d9-729c-537f-8f3f-c589e469e385" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322483}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "643e793d-c597-5a91-8005-379a40c51f62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322479} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4e73fe3-40ad-5897-b5c6-203a444a305e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322478}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f1ddbb09-cf38-595e-98d2-e5b17d8ef0c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-337362" :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y=({x}^{2}-1)\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4x).\n$$\n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, which of the following is a suitable ansatz for the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+2{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322484 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5fb547a-2518-5db9-b1b1-aab8d80328af" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order Linear ODEs Using VOP When the Forcing Term is Part of the Complementary Solution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9002d45b-9d8f-578e-a60b-15c24302564d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322486} {:answer/id #uuid "5022e34b-3eb8-54c0-b8b6-4b67a4472e98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322487} {:answer/id #uuid "29e186fa-1b2c-594c-93e2-0cc194a2133c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322488} {:answer/id #uuid "5c43d78b-e262-54f3-9558-26c56304fb86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322489} {:answer/id #uuid "52d11c94-b79c-59db-88ea-5b09afb3cbce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322490}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "52d11c94-b79c-59db-88ea-5b09af