wer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322501} {:answer/id #uuid "851a221c-4355-5c73-9199-a7c41e8fc923" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322502}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "851a221c-4355-5c73-9199-a7c41e8fc923" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322502} :answer-field/id #uuid "35b1fa1c-7c5f-50f2-a9e1-2886f8233585" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322498}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "60c74d98-62e0-535f-ae2c-bd22a02ff3d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-337466" :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4y={e}^{-4x}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+4{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-4x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-4x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{-4x})}^{′}+4(u{e}^{-4x}) & ={e}^{-4x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-4x}-4u{e}^{-4x}+4u{e}^{-4x} & ={e}^{-4x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-4x}-\\cancel{4u{e}^{-4x}}+\\cancel{4u{e}^{-4x}} & ={e}^{-4x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-4x} & ={e}^{-4x} \\\\ {u}^{′} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =1 \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{-4x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{-4x} \\\\  & =x{e}^{-4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322503 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5fb547a-2518-5db9-b1b1-aab8d80328af" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order Linear ODEs Using VOP When the Forcing Term is Part of the Complementary Solution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83b2a387-153e-5713-acb2-2d9eb8a6c3e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322505} {:answer/id #uuid "d93b35aa-a255-5f3a-b92e-9a4884102d24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322506} {:answer/id #uuid "8c579bd9-165b-54a5-afa3-c14216f2ace2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322507} {:answer/id #uuid "a1c6a8f3-2457-5556-be60-f9bbb638cc22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-7x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322508} {:answer/id #uuid "df338423-9736-55a3-8c81-85e4ebcd91e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{7x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322509}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/