651a157-9404-56b4-851c-c3b23e4315cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-7x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322520} {:answer/id #uuid "0f0c6c53-1491-5590-a760-4fa8c8600e50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{e}^{7x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322521}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e6e88ae0-40b5-5444-b469-bfe965901fb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{e}^{-7x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322518} :answer-field/id #uuid "b883cf93-0b66-598b-9c8d-150823fb8aee" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322517}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "34254624-9c90-5339-836d-8f0fcd78b2d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-337469" :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+7y={e}^{-7x}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+7{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-7x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-7x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{-7x})}^{′}+7(u{e}^{-7x}) & ={e}^{-7x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-7x}-7u{e}^{-7x}+7u{e}^{-7x} & ={e}^{-7x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-7x}-\\cancel{7u{e}^{-7x}}+\\cancel{7u{e}^{-7x}} & ={e}^{-7x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-7x} & ={e}^{-7x} \\\\ {u}^{′} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =1 \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{-7x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{-7x} \\\\  & =x{e}^{-7x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322522 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5fb547a-2518-5db9-b1b1-aab8d80328af" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order Linear ODEs Using VOP When the Forcing Term is Part of the Complementary Solution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "72456b48-e075-5fe1-aa7e-9a04a84c0ebc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-8x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322524} {:answer/id #uuid "90c2e1fa-1604-553d-b2f0-856ad232156a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322525} {:answer/id #uuid "a01ea5b4-24e7-5c53-918e-c81fbc9dbc2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{8x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322526} {:answer/id #uuid "bf4cb766-d883-5b1e-adec-bb846a0f298c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{12x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322527} {:answer/id #uuid "8d4c1102-6c98-51e5-be16-44bb947b1ceb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322528}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "90c2e1fa-1604-553d-b2f0-856ad232156a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322525} :answer-field/id #uuid "943809b7-19a0-5707-b895-5b212b84b6d1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322523} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dbfb77dd-15bb-5b6a-91a7-0a2514ecdbe1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6x-\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{-8x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322530} {:answer/id #uuid "6dbe9943-b403-5b1b-8462-77d8b89d8bab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{3}{2}x{e}^{-8x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322531} {:answer/id #uuid "868935d0-e49f-5a6e-bf52-f103c83697de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{3}{4}x{e}^{-8x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322532} {:answer/id #uuid "0496c7e0-37f8-5d13-8326-242f7bffe934" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6x-\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{-8x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322533}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0496c7e0-37f8-5d13-8326-242f7bffe934" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6x-\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{-8x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322533} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7a27eaf-57ee-53aa-ab93-27d8993f107c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322529} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c1892694-8b47-5cf5-80a5-e72868e32a4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{4x}-\\frac{3}{2}x{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322535} {:answer/id #uuid "ff71f8bd-f6db-5836-85e8-8c8f6758cbed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{4x}-\\frac{3}{4}x{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322536} {:answer/id #uuid "b81bf50c-18af-56fe-9e92-a8337b44c37e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6x{e}^{4x}-\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322537} {:answer/id #uuid "e9a79a5c-4ce4-5635-b02f-41349e0916c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6x{e}^{4x}-\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322538}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b81bf50c-18af-56fe-9e92-a8337b44c37e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6x{e}^{4x}-\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322537} :answer-field/id #uuid "4cba6432-298f-5f3e-829c-84ecba257d9e" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322534}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "65f54963-6769-575e-9f24-5929927fa99b" :question/math-academy-id "q-337510" :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-4y=12\\operatorname{cosh}⁡4x.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-4{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{4x})}^{′}-4(u{e}^{4x}) & =12\\operatorname{cosh}⁡4x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{4x}+4u{e}^{4x}-4u{e}^{4x} & =12\\operatorname{cosh}⁡4x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{4x}+\\cancel{4u{e}^{4x}}-\\cancel{4u{e}^{4x}} & =12\\operatorname{cosh}⁡4x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{4x} & =12\\operatorname{cosh}⁡4x \\\\ {u}^{′} & =12{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡4x=\\frac{{e}^{4x}+{e}^{-4x}}{2}\n$$\n\nour equation for $u$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =12{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡4x \\\\  & =12{e}^{-4x}(\\frac{{e}^{4x}+{e}^{-4x}}{2}) \\\\  & =6{e}^{-4x}({e}^{4x}+{e}^{-4x}) \\\\  & =6+6{e}^{-8x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =6+6{e}^{-8x} \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 6+6{e}^{-8x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =6x-\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{-8x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{4x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{4x} \\\\  & =6x{e}^{4x}-\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{-4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322539 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5fb547a-2518-5db9-b1b1-aab8d80328af" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order Linear ODEs Using VOP When the Forcing Term is Part of the Complementary Solution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f71635c7-e6ec-5fbd-8a22-57ee161ff15f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322541} {:answer/id #uuid "39513689-64f0-507b-9ca5-a70dea0dffbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322542} {:answer/id #uuid "fd732bac-94e8-5ac3-a14a-e7de0