#edb/ref 17592186322552} {:answer/id #uuid "309e96d2-66a9-529a-8ff8-be636cf45487" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2x}+x{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322553} {:answer/id #uuid "eff57f56-b318-53f6-968f-23839a93c6a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x{e}^{2x}+2{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322554} {:answer/id #uuid "1b3cfcfc-bddf-5ffc-99bf-19ba7ada45b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x{e}^{2x}+{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322555}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b3cfcfc-bddf-5ffc-99bf-19ba7ada45b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x{e}^{2x}+{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322555} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a9ae686-fb95-5b55-9227-fb6e4e71c2e6" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322551}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "777ddb07-aadb-556e-9de0-b0dc9c5809ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-337518" :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2y=8\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-2{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{2x})}^{′}-2(u{e}^{2x}) & =8\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{2x}+2u{e}^{2x}-2u{e}^{2x} & =8\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{2x}+\\cancel{2u{e}^{2x}}-\\cancel{2u{e}^{2x}} & =8\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{2x} & =8\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x \\\\ {u}^{′} & =8{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x=\\frac{{e}^{2x}-{e}^{-2x}}{2}\n$$\n\nour equation for $u$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =8{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x \\\\  & =8{e}^{-2x}(\\frac{{e}^{2x}-{e}^{-2x}}{2}) \\\\  & =4{e}^{-2x}({e}^{2x}-{e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =4-4{e}^{-4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =4-4{e}^{-4x} \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 4-4{e}^{-4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =4x+{e}^{-4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{2x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{2x} \\\\  & =4x{e}^{2x}+{e}^{-2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322556 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7ac34a18-053d-5eab-8c2c-bd0f15658186" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order Linear ODEs Using Variation of Parameters"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53382c9c-8a39-5fd7-aad0-552180ad4298" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322558} {:answer/id #uuid "39c33438-9102-5aac-8846-8bae71eca5fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322559} {:answer/id #uuid "54d88ef0-316a-5bd7-ad34-6fd868dc1d23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322560} {:answer/id #uuid "4beb7f87-00b7-5e9f-ba45-e98171b40d16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322561} {:answer/id #uuid "bf41dca0-2d07-5362-ad81-c525b027914e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322562}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "39c33438-9102-5aac-8846-8bae71eca5fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322559} :answer-field/id #uuid "3caac505-b71f-59b4-9208-cd6f0956283c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322557} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ea21ccea-5522-566f-bfac-03661a736fb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322564} {:answer/id #uuid "5267f9d4-ecdf-5942-ab35-f2f7c794a4e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322565} {:answer/id #uuid "147fdcdb-59bf-5eec-b9a9-03b3cc108392" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322566} {:answer/id #uuid "ae96b78f-d720-5fbd-b19c-3d4db51652bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322567} {:answer/id #uuid "d2bee5b9-862b-5a3d-a5cd-550f8b9dbafa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322568}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d2bee5b9-862b-5a3d-a5cd-550f8b9dbafa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322568} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a63d83e-bb88-575b-a0fd-da481136a77c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322563} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04ae7f96-8a06-5107-b97f-5cc9fab774e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322570} {:answer/id #uuid "9f814402-6c71-53e1-b21e-b86114f0aeac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322571} {:answer/id #uuid "48c95440-f966-52f2-a676-1e699b6fa825" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322572} {:answer/id #uuid "ae5bdf20-d5e0-5db4-9307-2356669ff69a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322573} {:answer/id #uuid "18b29a18-cadc-534e-87a2-98c19e87b76c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322574}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f814402-6c71-53e1-b21e-b86114f0aeac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322571} :answer-field/id #uuid "3cc3eeb4-8fbc-5444-a26c-0bcb52acdb1d" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322569}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "efe05efa-2761-5181-aebf-f550bba3f8de" :question/math-academy-id "q-352890" :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3y={e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-3{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{3x})}^{′}-3(u{e}^{3x}) & ={e}^{2x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{3x}+3u{e}^{3x}-3u{e}^{3x} & ={e}^{2x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{3x}+\\cancel{3u{e}^{3x}}-\\cancel{3u{e}^{3x}} & ={e}^{2x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{3x} & ={e}^{2x} \\\\ {u}^{′} & ={e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & ={e}^{-x} \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =-{e}^{-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{3x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{3x} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-x}\\cdot {e}^{3x} \\\\  & =-{e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322575 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7ac34a18-053d-5eab-8c2c-bd0f15658186" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order Linear ODEs Using Variation of Parameters"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5632ee24-3c17-549c-92eb-bec53ec66345" :answer/type {:d