:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322585} :answer-field/id #uuid "984a7474-b6a2-5043-a90b-bbd19794707c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322582} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f43e954-574e-502a-877c-4c16ada55b6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{e}^{-5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322589} {:answer/id #uuid "165be3dc-14b2-5c84-909d-a50f14f13234" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{e}^{-5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322590} {:answer/id #uuid "1cb5dbc5-663d-5b8f-b85a-19ceff1f8fb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{-5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322591} {:answer/id #uuid "1b2c773e-9533-5140-9fb2-b5794668210f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{e}^{-5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322592} {:answer/id #uuid "586f09db-6b5e-578a-81e5-54426aaa5c2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322593}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "165be3dc-14b2-5c84-909d-a50f14f13234" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{e}^{-5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322590} :answer-field/id #uuid "2395c858-1801-51a8-9656-5294ed8b6cd6" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322588}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "94c1a434-d90c-5a8b-b10b-a3af3883d298" :question/math-academy-id "q-352891" :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y=6{e}^{-5x}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+2{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{-2x})}^{′}+2(u{e}^{-2x}) & =6{e}^{-5x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-2x}-2u{e}^{-2x}+2u{e}^{-2x} & =6{e}^{-5x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-2x}-\\cancel{2u{e}^{-2x}}+\\cancel{2u{e}^{-2x}} & =6{e}^{-5x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-2x} & =6{e}^{-5x} \\\\ {u}^{′} & =6{e}^{-3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =6{e}^{-3x} \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 6{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =-2{e}^{-3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{-2x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{-2x} \\\\  & =-2{e}^{-3x}\\cdot {e}^{-2x} \\\\  & =-2{e}^{-5x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322594 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7ac34a18-053d-5eab-8c2c-bd0f15658186" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order Linear ODEs Using Variation of Parameters"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e592a8d3-17e8-59c7-afe6-4cc9d2c43f5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322596} {:answer/id #uuid "83aa7265-d0b5-5528-a307-8cf58fe6fe10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322597} {:answer/id #uuid "c9bdd279-d569-57b3-b8b1-f7ba9b5a920b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322598} {:answer/id #uuid "9d2fa23d-873d-5c39-8980-f191059da23f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322599} {:answer/id #uuid "01a71ab3-1b40-59e2-82c3-17a21226d020" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322600}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d2fa23d-873d-5c39-8980-f191059da23f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322599} :answer-field/id #uuid "48a936a0-515e-59af-9108-c2d2d81861cf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322595} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0270e18d-3f1b-5a95-9e83-eb980ce79a25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int {e}^{-3x}(1-4x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322602} {:answer/id #uuid "801ee798-c302-5df3-8f62-5f7b4033a3bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int {e}^{3x}(1-4x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322603} {:answer/id #uuid "edea84b9-9a06-5dd2-b2a3-3fbc6ba0ec9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int {e}^{3x}(4x-1)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322604} {:answer/id #uuid "c7ae9dd2-b550-5283-982f-5afc7fc5b34d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int {e}^{3x}(1+4x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322605}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "801ee798-c302-5df3-8f62-5f7b4033a3bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int {e}^{3x}(1-4x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322603} :answer-field/id #uuid "2439fbbe-de49-568c-be29-c901f5d6b6dd" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322601} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b89e869-1375-56ec-b08a-36d0250e080a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{3x}\\int {e}^{-3x}(1-4x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322607} {:answer/id #uuid "81007bfb-0086-5639-8b0c-ef7e687776a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{3x}\\int {e}^{3x}(1-4x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322608} {:answer/id #uuid "ec89555e-7efc-5a2a-bf4a-0d4b08559d6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-3x}\\int {e}^{-3x}(1-4x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322609} {:answer/id #uuid "1a1dfe17-d38d-5f0a-956d-e6345839c7a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-3x}\\int {e}^{3x}(1-4x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322610}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a1dfe17-d38d-5f0a-956d-e6345839c7a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-3x}\\int {e}^{3x}(1-4x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322610} :answer-field/id #uuid "530b8a1d-784c-59ed-a7dc-86f1c19e2524" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322606}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d21173e6-c5c3-5364-8e5b-d645bc7f05bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-352895" :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+3y=1-4x.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+3{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{-3x})}^{′}+3(u{e}^{-3x}) & =1-4x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-3x}-3u{e}^{-3x}+3u{e}^{-3x} & =1-4x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-3x}-\\cancel{3u{e}^{-3x}}+\\cancel{3u{e}^{-3x}} & =1-4x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-3x} & =1-4x \\\\ {u}^{′} & ={e}^{3x}(1-4x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & ={e}^{3x}(1-4x) \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{3x}(1-4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =\\int {e}^{3x}(1-4x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{-3x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{-3x} \\\\  & =\\int {e}^{3x}(1-4x)\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {e}^{-3x} \\\\  & ={e}^{-3x}\\int {e}^{3x}(1-4x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
